# Camera paths (design §4.1), and the cases a camera path can be in.
#
# The geometry is phase3.map's, and deliberately so: it already contains the two
# kinds of moving sector, and a camera path's one interesting relationship with
# the simulation is which of them it is bolted to. What this map adds is the
# `path` blocks, which exist nowhere else.
#
#   sector 0   the yard: static ground, where the static paths live
#   sector 1   a parametric lift. Rises 3 units on a fixed schedule, so a client
#              computes its position for any tick from parameters sent once --
#              which is what makes a path anchored here predictable.
#   sector 2   a scripted-discrete mover. Its offset is STREAMED every snapshot
#              and has no closed form, so a path anchored here judders at the
#              snapshot rate. Authored on purpose, as the thing the overlay
#              colours differently and docs/MAP_AUTHOR.md tells you not to do.
#   sector 3   a crusher, unused by the paths; it keeps the geometry identical
#              to phase3 so the two can be compared.
#
#   y
#   ^   +--------+--------+
#   |   |   2    |   3    |   discrete mover | crusher
#   |   +--------+--------+
#   |   |   0    |   1    |   yard | parametric lift
#   |   +--------+--------+
#   +-------------------------> x

sector floor 0 ceil 12 tag 1
  vertex  0   0   wall
  vertex 20   0   neighbor 1
  vertex 20  20   neighbor 2
  vertex  0  20   wall
end

# tag 100+N: a parametric lift rising N units.
sector floor 0 ceil 12 tag 103
  vertex 20   0   wall
  vertex 40   0   wall
  vertex 40  20   neighbor 3
  vertex 20  20   neighbor 0
end

# tag 400+N: a scripted-discrete mover with N units of range.
sector floor 0 ceil 12 tag 402
  vertex  0  20   neighbor 0
  vertex 20  20   neighbor 3
  vertex 20  40   wall
  vertex  0  40   wall
end

# tag 200+N: a crusher whose ceiling drops N units.
sector floor 0 ceil 12 tag 211
  vertex 20  20   neighbor 1
  vertex 40  20   wall
  vertex 40  40   wall
  vertex 20  40   neighbor 2
end

spawn yard      10 10 0
spawn lift      30 10 0
spawn drifter   10 30 0
spawn crusher   30 30 0

# --- camera paths -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# `waypoint` is the same word a rail uses, because it is the same thing: an
# anchored, sector-relative point. Which block is open decides where it goes.
#
# A path is NOT a rail. Rails are §7.4's -- simulated, hashed, ridden by an
# entity, branching at junctions Lua chooses between. Nothing here branches and
# nothing in the simulation reads any of it.

# 1: STATIC, and bent. Chords of 16 and 6 meeting at a right angle -- the shape
# that separates a curve from its polyline most sharply, so this is the path the
# arc-length agreement between mapc and spline.js is measured on.
path tag 1
  waypoint  2  2 1
  waypoint 18  2 1
  waypoint 18  8 1
end

# 2: ANCHORED TO THE PARAMETRIC LIFT (sector 1, x 20..40 y 0..20). Every point
# rides it, so the path translates rigidly and predictably. This is the case
# that earns camera paths a place in the map file rather than in a script.
path tag 2
  waypoint 24  4 1
  waypoint 36  4 1
  waypoint 36 16 1
end

# 3: A LOOP. Four points around the yard, so a flythrough set going on it never
# clamps and a full lap returns exactly to its start.
path tag 3 loop
  waypoint  3  3 6
  waypoint 17  3 6
  waypoint 17 17 6
  waypoint  3 17 6
end

# 4: TWO POINTS. The minimum a path can be, and a straight line -- the control
# for the arc-length test, because smoothing that lengthened this one would be
# measuring something other than arc length.
path tag 4
  waypoint  2 12 2
  waypoint 18 12 2
end

# 5 and 6, BOTH TAGGED 5. Tags are not unique and nothing enforces it, so the
# rule has to be written down and tested rather than assumed: first match wins,
# which is the lower index. Without a map that contains the ambiguity, a
# pathIndexForTag that returned the LAST match would pass every other test.
path tag 5
  waypoint  4 24 1
  waypoint 16 24 1
end

path tag 5
  waypoint  4 36 1
  waypoint 16 36 1
end

# 7: ANCHORED TO THE DISCRETE MOVER (sector 2, x 0..20 y 20..40). The path whose
# points arrive over the network at half the tick rate. Authored so the failure
# mode has a fixture rather than only a warning in the docs.
path tag 7
  waypoint  6 26 1
  waypoint 14 26 1
  waypoint 14 34 1
end
